erity of the crime  that decision to seek the death penalty  has been ratified by harold pryor who is  mr sassa's successor in this case there  are 17 victims of homicide committed by  this defendant over here this is a case  that calls out for the death penalty and  the death penalty is appropriate and it  is not being sought at the whim of one  man and the defendant's motion should be  denied  in the end judge elizabeth schreyer  ruled that the death penalty will be a  possible sentence for cruz she also  knocked down a defense request that  victim impact statements not be shown  publicly  let's bring in criminal defense attorney  taylor cost and co-host terry austin to  discuss cruz and the death penalty terry  it's a last but maybe not last-ditch  effort to take the death penalty off the  table what'd you think of the defense's  argument to remove the death penalty  there's no question the defense's  arguments were extremely weak look we  have a defendant who has pled guilty to  17 counts of murder and 17 counts of  attempted murder no question this is a  death penalty case the defense tried to  claim that the death penalty was  arbitrarily applied by the state no  matter what you think about past cases  this was not arbitrarily applied the  defense also tried to say that the  targeted school did not disrupt a public  function that was one of the aggravating  factors well it did disrupt a  governmental function so the judge  didn't buy any defense arguments and i  think that was absolutely the right  decision now taylor i think i can guess  what your answer may be but let me try  it anyways was the prosecution making  the right arguments here to keep the  death penalty off the table or was it  something else  yeah listen you know the death penalty  and the application of the death penalty  is a fairly simple but two-step process  the first step is does the case meet the  statutory requirements for the death  penalty very easy right and the second  step is very simple it's called  prosecutorial discretion okay each case  has to be  looked at  you have to assess each and every  mitigating and aggravating factor you  have to look each case from each other  and the district attorney an elected  official ultimately gets to make the  decision what penalty they will seek  it's not up to them to impose the  penalty it's just up to them to make a  decision whether or not they're going to  seek it and it is important that  prosecutors use this discretion because  there shouldn't be a blanket policy it  should be each individual case assessed  for strength weaknesses aggravating and  mitigating factors i would agree if  there's any case where the death penalty  may fit i can see why the argument would  be for this one of all other cases still  ahead on law and crime daily we head to  the west coast where convicted murderer  scott peterson is re-sentenced when we  come back hear from both sides about  peterson's potential retrial  [Music]  welcome back to law and crime daily  convicted wife killer scott peterson is  resentenced to life in prison without  parole for the murder of his wife lacey  and unborn son on christmas eve 2002.  peterson wore a red jail jumpsuit during  the hearing wednesday tv cameras were  not allowed but still photos capture the  hearing lacey peterson's mother sharon  rocha and siblings spoke sharon rocha  said quote no matter what happens you  will always be their murderer and  outline the circumstantial evidence in  the case peterson could get at a new  trial if the judge finds a juror  intentionally lied to get on the jury  the 49-year-old was expected to speak at  the hearing but judge and christian  masolo didn't allow it because the life  sentence was mandatory peterson's  attorney and sister-in-law spoke after  the hearing ending  i think he just also wanted to reach out  and tell the rocha family that he  understands  their feelings and he understands why  they believe  that he is  guilty but he wanted to make it clear  that there is no way he could have  possibly harmed lacey and connor this is  a capital murder trial he is in prison  for murdering his wife and unborn son  there is no forensic evidence there is  no timeline to this crime scott peterson  is innocent  a hearing on the jury misconduct claim  will be held next february  taylor in a case with so many twists and  turns of technical arguments  what do you think of the judge stopping  peterson from speaking and could that  create some sort of a pellet issue  i have to be honest i don't understand  it um you know regardless of the fact  that the sentence was mandatory you know  we we are certainly as a country used to  defendants being able to speak at their  own sentence even if it's to just so  show some remorse i understand this case  is riddled with a ton of history but i  just don't see the upside in refusing to  let him speak i think it's a potential  error yeah now terry peterson is still  waiting for a new trial what's next for  him uh after the sentencing  you know peterson is going to have to  wait and see it was his team that moved  for this new trial they're claiming that  there was prejudicial misconduct as far  as juror number seven was concerned  they're saying that rochelle niece did  not disclose that she was a victim of  domestic violence she didn't disclose  that she had a restraining order against  her boyfriend's ex-girlfriend and so  there's also this new evidence that they  are claiming that lacey peterson was  killed when she stumbled upon you know  the burglary so i think in the meantime  until this hearing in late february his  attorneys are going to be trying to put  together evidence for this hearing and  trying to make sure that they have  enough to convince the judge that he  should get a new trial frankly i don't  think it's going to work and i think the  public out war will be huge because  there's no question based on the  evidence at the trial that he committed  this crime so we'll see what happens all  right sounds like the end of the chapter  but not the end of the story for scott  peterson thank you both and thank you  for joining us here on law and crime  daily we'll see you next time as we  discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

